{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2145 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f7ab91e8d9839264245bed1edb341e730b861dc908e6ae3aeacc5d8dbc3c4a91" :edb/db-before-t 2144 :edb/db-after-t 2145 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535457 50 #inst "2026-10-14T02:27:05.882Z" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507405 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507412 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186054772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507499 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507438 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507486 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507433 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507410 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507455 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507404 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507442 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507457 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066547 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066548 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066549 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066550 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066551 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506861 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506876 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507146 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507150 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186066546 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507193 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186066546 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507150 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186068685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507422 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186068686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507425 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186068687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507497 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186068687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507522 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186068935 1050 480.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186069228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186069228 1071 "What is the solution to $-10 < \\frac{6 - 2x}{3} < 0$?" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186069228 1071 "What is the solution to $-10<\\frac{6-2x}{3}<0?$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186069228 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & <\\frac{6-2x}{3}<0 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-10) & <3\\cdot (\\frac{6-2x}{3})<3\\cdot 0 \\\\ -30 & <6-2x<0 \\\\ -30-6 & <6-2x-6<0-6 \\\\ -36 & <-2x<-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-2.$ However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36 & <-2x<-6 \\\\ \\frac{-36}{-2} & >\\frac{-2x}{-2}>\\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\ 18 & >x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $18>x>3,$ but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n3<x<18\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186076850 1078 #edb/ref 17592186076855 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186076868 1078 #edb/ref 17592186076872 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078220 1071 "Given that the function\n![](images/f7/f72fd02a0488c44ac7472487222507c5739853cd25720502a726844e0c23c915.svg)\nis continuous at $x=0,$ find the value of the constant $b.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078220 1071 "Given that the function\n${\\sqrt{2b - 3x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ -x + 2,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078220 1074 "At $x=0$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{-})\\sqrt{2b - 3x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2b - 3(0)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})(-x + 2) \\\\\n&= - (0) + 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) \\\\\n2 &= \\sqrt{2b} \\\\\n4 &= 2b \\\\\nb &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078220 1074 "At $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\sqrt{2b-3x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2b-3(0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(-x+2) \\\\  & =-(0)+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 2 & =\\sqrt{2b} \\\\ 4 & =2b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078222 1071 "Find the value(s) of the constant $a$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=2$.\n${(2x + a)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 2 \\\\ x^{2} - 3,\\end{vmatrix}x > 2$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078222 1071 "Find the value(s) of the constant $a$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n![](images/be/bee9ecfc2bcfaa7741ed1c2aca90b8a077c19715c2a642a9622856f986f434f3.svg)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078222 1074 "At $x=2$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})(2x + a)^{2} \\\\\n&= (2(2) + a)^{2} \\\\\n&= (4 + a)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})(x^{2} - 3) \\\\\n&= (2)^{2} - 3 \\\\\n&= 4 - 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) \\\\\n(4 + a)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(a + 4)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n\\mid a + 4 \\mid &= 1 \\\\\na + 4 &= ± 1 \\\\\na &= -4 ± 1 \\\\\na &= -5,-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=-5$ or $a=-3$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078222 1074 "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2x+a)^{2} \\\\  & =(2(2)+a)^{2} \\\\  & =(4+a)^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ (4+a)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |a+4| & =1 \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ a & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ a & =-5,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=-5$ or $a=-3.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078225 1071 "Given that the function\n![](images/47/47e2bbdd0741bbac490c3ac0851e95b2103078e22d06d4a369712236529e8302.svg) \nis continuous at $x=1$, find the value of $a.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078225 1071 "Given that the function\n${\\log_{2} (ax + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ \\log_{3} (2x + 1),\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\nis continuous at $x=1$, find the value of $a$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078225 1074 "At $x=1$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{-})\\log_{2} (ax + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (a(1) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})(\\log_{3} (2x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (2(1) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (3) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) \\\\\n\\log_{2} (a + 1) &= 1 \\\\\na + 1 &= 2^{1} \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078225 1074 "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(ax+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(a(1)+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(a+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(2(1)+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(a+1) & =1 \\\\ a+1 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078269 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is given below. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $y=f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\n![](images/d5/d524aa081eec487a6107a3685f87ec97fe6895f1c3b7a91bc4bb07a637f76f40.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078269 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is given below. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $y=f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\n![](images/d5/d524aa081eec487a6107a3685f87ec97fe6895f1c3b7a91bc4bb07a637f76f40.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078269 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$, then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b)$.\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$, then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b)$.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$, then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b)$.\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that $f'(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,-2)$, $(0,2)$ and $(5,\\infty)$:\n\n![](images/d5/d51ade5596e25a7ba791a3090689a0eacc52e6253ea894257be7e62c71deafa4.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is decreasing when $x\\in (-5,-2) \\cup (0,2) \\cup (5,\\infty)$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078269 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If $f'(x) <            0$ for all $x \\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that $f'(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,-2),$ $(0,2)$ and $(5,\\infty)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d5/d51ade5596e25a7ba791a3090689a0eacc52e6253ea894257be7e62c71deafa4.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is decreasing when $x\\in (-5,-2) \\cup (0,2) \\cup (5,\\infty).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078270 1071 "Let $v(t)$ denote the speed (in $\\textrm{km/h}$) of a hurricane at time $t$ (in hours) since the hurricane originated in the Atlantic Ocean. The graph of $y=v'(t)$, the derivative of $v(t)$, is given below. Find all the values of $t$ where the speed of the hurricane is decreasing.\n\n![](images/9e/9e93a8c0657797100f23b229be1a41c2c377a1711b33b4fc51fc44c8b5b03f89.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078270 1071 "Let $v(t)$ denote the speed (in $\\textrm{km/h}$) of a hurricane at time $t$ (in hours) since the hurricane originated in the Atlantic Ocean. The graph of $y=v'(t),$ the derivative of $v(t),$ is given below. Find all the values of $t$ where the speed of the hurricane is decreasing. \n\n![](images/9e/9e93a8c0657797100f23b229be1a41c2c377a1711b33b4fc51fc44c8b5b03f89.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078270 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $v'(t) = 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b)$, then $v(t)$ is constant on $(a,b)$.\n- When $v'(t) > 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b)$, then $v(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b)$.\n- When $v'(t) < 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b)$, then $v(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b)$.\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that $v'(t)$ is negative on the intervals $[0,2)$ and $(7,8]$:\n\n![](images/e2/e2f4f59a5ea966ec9927caed5a870c57d63b19654330ced524998787f5837e49.png)\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hurricane is decreasing when $t \\in [0,2) \\cup (7,8]$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078270 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $v'(t) = 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is constant on $(a,b).$\n\n- When $v'(t) > 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n- When $v'(t) <             0$ for all $t \\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that $v'(t)$ is negative on the intervals $[0,2)$ and $(7,8]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e2/e2f4f59a5ea966ec9927caed5a870c57d63b19654330ced524998787f5837e49.png)\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hurricane is decreasing when $t \\in [0,2) \\cup (7,8].$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078271 1071 "The graph of $y=z'(x)$, the derivative of $z(x)$, is given below. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y=z'(x)$ are shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/a3/a3639ee18c26d382ff8fd626f43873571fd0fffd8271228b8b1935c4d01c8212.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078271 1071 "The graph of $y=z'(x),$ the derivative of $z(x),$ is given below. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y=z'(x)$ are shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/a3/a3639ee18c26d382ff8fd626f43873571fd0fffd8271228b8b1935c4d01c8212.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078271 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $z'(x)=0$ or $z'(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If $z'(x)>0,$ then $z(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $z'(x)<         0,$ then $z(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $z'(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- $z'(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,8)$ | $8$ | $(8,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $p$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=3,$ $x=4,$ and $x=5.$  \n\nNotice that $x=8$ is *not* a relative extremum because the sign of $z'(x)$ does not change either side of this critical point." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078271 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $z'(x)=0$ or $z'(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n- If $z'(x)>0$, then $z(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $z'(x)< 0$, then $z(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $z'(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n- $z'(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,8)$ | $8$ | $(8,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $p$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=3$, $x=4$, and $x=5$.\n\nNotice that $x=8$ is *not* a relative extremum because the sign of $z'(x)$ does not change either side of this critical point." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078334 1071 "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=4$.\n${4^{cx + 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 4 \\\\ 4x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 4$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078334 1071 "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=4.$\n![](images/6e/6ed78555d5936e679b9aecd838d8b07f95031afe831eb3ef3e7cb64c5160f7c1.svg)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186078334 1074 "At $x=4$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{-})h(x) &= h(4) \\\\\n&= 4^{c(4) + 1} \\\\\n&= 4^{4c + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{+})h(x) &= lim_(x → 4^{+})(4x) \\\\\n&= 4(4) \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4^{4c + 1} &= 16 \\\\\n4^{4c + 1} &= 4^{2} \\\\\n4c + 1 &= 2 \\\\\n4c &= 1 \\\\\nc &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ for\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186078334 1074 "At $x=4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x) & =h(4) \\\\  & ={4}^{c(4)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4c+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(4x) \\\\  & =4(4) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{4c+1} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{4c+1} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 4c+1 & =2 \\\\ 4c & =1 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ for $c = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$" 1