unction\n$g(x) = {\\sqrt{b - 3x} + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ x + 3,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$\nis continuous at $x = 0$, find the value of the constant $b$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186079012 1074 "At $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{b-3(0)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{b}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(x+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{b}+2 & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{b} & =3-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186079015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186079015 1071 "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x = 2$.\n$g(x) = {3^{2ax + 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 2 \\\\ 2x + 5,\\end{vmatrix}x > 2$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186079015 1071 "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2ax+1}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ 2x+5, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186079015 1074 "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\  & ={3}^{2a(2)+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{4a+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=2,$ the right-sided limit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+5) \\\\  & =2(2)+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4a+1} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{4a+1} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 4a+1 & =2 \\\\ 4a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186079683 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} + kx,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 3, \\\\ x^{3},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 3\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-3)^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → (-3)^{-})(3x^{2} + kx) \\\\\n&= 3(-3)^{2} + k(-3) \\\\\n&= 27 - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-3)^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → (-3)^{+})x^{3} \\\\\n&= (-3)^{3} \\\\\n&=-27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27 - 3k &= -27 \\\\\n3k &= 54 \\\\\nk &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3$, we need to choose $k=18$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186079683 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/e4/e46fcaf79d93513c8b946de51ef451ad8a638936681ce3fc70ffdbe8de023078.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(3{x}^{2}+kx) \\\\  & =3(-3)^{2}+k(-3) \\\\  & =27-3k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =(-3)^{3} \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3k & =-27 \\\\ 3k & =54 \\\\ k & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3,$ we need to choose $k=18.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186079698 1064 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a differentiable function. The graph of the derivative $y=f'(x)$ is given below. Can we determine the intervals on which $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing?\n\n![](images/5b/5ba6b1945470b0ad98c1d5b6fe909e09759fb55f5ec5444c06079404c53853ad.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we use the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is positive for every value in $(a,b).$ *\n\nWe also have the following related theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f(x)$ is decreasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is negative for every value in $(a,b).$ *\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $f'(x) > 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(0,4), \\quad (7,10), \\quad (10,15).\n$$\n\nAlso from the graph, we see that $f'(x) < 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(4,7), \\quad (15, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \\in (0,4) \\cup (7,10) \\cup (10,15)$ and decreasing for $x \\in (4,7) \\cup (15, \\infty).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186079698 1064 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a differentiable function. The graph of the derivative $y=f'(x)$ is given below. Can we determine the intervals on which $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing?\n\n![](images/5b/5ba6b1945470b0ad98c1d5b6fe909e09759fb55f5ec5444c06079404c53853ad.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we use the following theorem:\n\n> *A differentiable function $f(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is positive for every value in $(a,b)$.*\n\nWe also have the following related theorem:\n\n> *A differentiable function $f(x)$ is decreasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is negative for every value in $(a,b)$.*\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $f'(x) > 0$ on the intervals\n$(0,4), \\quad (7,10), \\quad (10,15)$.\n\nAlso from the graph, we see that $f'(x) < 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(4,7), \\quad (15, \\infty)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \\in (0,4) \\cup (7,10) \\cup (10,15)$ and decreasing for $x \\in (4,7) \\cup (15, \\infty)$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186079699 1064 "Recall that we can use the first derivative test to identify the relative extrema of a continuous function $f(x)$:\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *positive* to *negative* around $x=a$.![](images/99/9912cc68ed61884957907c360f172fe37582827b67e0b9873795ae475dc1e86c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *negative* to *positive* around $x=a$.![](images/b8/b8da20322f6797554f2c52642fd313df78ce1a7a67f54e3c524af446398ce515.png)\n\nSo, to determine the relative extrema of a function $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x)$, we need to pay attention to the locations where $f'(x)$ changes sign." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186079699 1064 "Recall that we can use the first derivative test to identify the relative extrema of a continuous function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *positive* to *negative* around $x=a.$\n\n![](images/99/9912cc68ed61884957907c360f172fe37582827b67e0b9873795ae475dc1e86c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *negative* to *positive* around $x=a.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8da20322f6797554f2c52642fd313df78ce1a7a67f54e3c524af446398ce515.png)\n\nSo, to determine the relative extrema of a function $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to the locations where $f'(x)$ changes sign." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186082775 1078 #edb/ref 17592186082776 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [175