alse] [17592186082940 1074 "The range of $f(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{ -1,0,2,3,4  \\}.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186082941 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $-1$. This occurs at the point $(-1,-1)$.\n- The highest $y$-value is $8$. This occurs at the point $(2,8)$.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $8$, including both $-1$ and $8$.\n\nTherefore, the range is\n\n$$\n-1\\leq y \\leq 8\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n-1\\leq f(x) \\leq 8\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186082941 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-1).$\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $8.$ This occurs at the point $(2,8).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $8,$ including both $-1$ and $8.$\n\nTherefore, the range is \n\n$$\n-1\\leq y \\leq 8,\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n-1\\leq f(x) \\leq 8.\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507409 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507440 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507490 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507444 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507489 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083444 1050 420.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083974 1071 "In a factory, cookies contain $15$ grams of sugar on average. However, $10%$ of cookies contain more than $17$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the percent of cookies that contain fewer than $12$ grams of sugar, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083974 1071 "In a factory, cookies contain $15$ grams of sugar on average. However, $10%$ of cookies contain more than $17$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the percent of cookies that contain fewer than $12$ grams of sugar, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution,*\n\n| $p$ | $0.900$ | $0.950$ | $0.975$ | $0.990$ | $0.995$ | $0.999$ | $0.9995$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $Φ^{-1}(p)$ | $1.2816$ | $1.6449$ | $1.9600$ | $2.3263$ | $2.5758$ | $3.0902$ | $3.2905$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1.9$ | $0.0287$ | $0.0281$ | $0.0274$ |\n| $-1.8$ | $0.0359$ | $0.0351$ | $0.0344$ |\n| $-1.7$ | $0.0446$ | $0.0436$ | $0.0427$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186083974 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of grams of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>17)=0.1.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =1-P(X>17) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =0.9 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{17-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)≈1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.6408 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.6408} \\\\  & ≈1.5605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12) & =P(X\\le 12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12-15}{1.5605}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.92) \\\\  & =0.0274 \\\\  & ≈2.7%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $2.7%$ of cookies contain less than $12$ grams of sugar." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083975 1071 "The test scores of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $5$. What is the probability that a randomly selected student got a score of $80$ or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.3$ | $0.9032$ | $0.9049$ | $0.9066$ |\n| $1.4$ | $0.9192$ | $0.9207$ | $0.9222$ |\n| $1.5$ | $0.9332$ | $0.9345$ | $0.9357$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186083975 1071 "The test scores of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $5.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student got a score of $80$ or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186083975 1074 "Let $X$ represent the score of a randomly selected student. Then we have $X∼N(73,{5}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 80).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 80) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{80-73}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1.4) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.4) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.40)=1-0.9192≈0.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student got $80$ or more is $0.08.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186084518 1064 "The **range** of a function is the collection of numbers (or set) containing all the function's outputs.\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$, defined by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/60/60ae5361f9f51bb7e61083269770ee3dd432e04ad2f968df07d78c8dc61e7188.png)\n\nIn a mapping diagram, the range consists of all the outputs to which an arrow is drawn. Therefore, the range is\n\n$$\n\\{-1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nThe value $y=0$ is not part of the range because there is no $x$-value such that\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186084518 1064 "The **range** of a function is the collection of numbers (or set) containing all the function's outputs.\n\nConsider the function $f(x),$ defined by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/60/60ae5361f9f51bb7e61083269770ee3dd432e04ad2f968df07d78c8dc61e7188.png)\n\n \n\nIn a mapping diagram, the range consists of all the outputs to which an arrow is drawn. Therefore, the range is \n\n$$\n\\{ -1,2,3 \\}.\n$$\n\nThe value $y=0$ is not part of the range because there is no $x$-value such that $f(x) = 0.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186084519 1064 "When we graph a function, the range is the set of all $y$-values given by the graph.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x\n$$\n\ngraphed below.\n\n![](images/09/09716693ba950bd3d14d0ea008dbd9d99dc51a26f30e87ccbf1559f34329fed9.png)\n\nThe graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty < y< \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n$-\\infty < f(x) < \\infty$.\n\nFurthermore, we can express the range as $f(x)\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ using interval notation.\n\nIf we restrict the domain, the range also changes. Let's now consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x, \\quad x \\in [0,2)\n$$\n\nThe graph $y = g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/791aca2b232c0eee5950c4c1c87073b4c51cd5d61d094de893ca4a2ec28e8d1e.png)\n\nThe $y$ values go from $y=0$ up to (but not including) $y=4$, including every value in between. Therefore, the range of the function is\n\n$$\n0\\leq g(x) < 4\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $g(x)\\in [0,4)$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186084519 1064 "When we graph a function, the range is the set of all $y$-values given by the graph.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function $f(x) = 2x,$ graphed below.\n\n![](images/09/09716693ba950bd3d14d0ea008dbd9d99dc51a26f30e87ccbf1559f34329fed9.png)\n\nThe graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty < y< \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n\n$$\n-\\infty < f(x) < \\infty.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we can express the range as $f(x)\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ using interval notation.\n\nIf we restrict the domain, the range also changes. Let's now consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x, \\quad x \\in [0,2).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y = g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/791aca2b232c0eee5950c4c1c87073b4c51cd5d61d094de893ca4a2ec28e8d1e.png)\n\nThe $y$ values go from $y=0$ up to (but not including) $y=4,$ including every value in between. Therefore, the range of the function is \n\n$$\n0\\leq g(x) < 4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $g(x)\\in [0,4).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186087728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507420 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186087728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507518 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186087729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507421 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186087729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507473 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186087921 1050 960.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186088045 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](images/2c/2ceb970a7a8f72eae28e41007a925a04f7f1e3eea5924af726912c60552ff14e.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186088045 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089454 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089455 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089456 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089457 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506694 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506734 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506920 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186089453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507166 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186089453 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506694 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092307 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507512 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507415 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507481 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507514 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092547 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=4.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186092547 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $5\\tanh x = 4$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186092547 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}) & =4 \\\\ 5({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =4({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\ 5{e}^{x}-5{e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & =9{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x}$ and solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 9{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{1/2}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only real solution is $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186095988 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095992 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507526 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507408 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507484 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186097344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186097344 1071 "![](images/68/68379dee045e8b5d4f123f91c0f8c953d2cbbe6475c5ca13d4435889343c625d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$ are given above. Find $h^{′}(-1.5)$ where $h(x) = f(x)g(x)$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186097344 1071 "![](images/68/68379dee045e8b5d4f123f91c0f8c953d2cbbe6475c5ca13d4435889343c625d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1.5)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186097344 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1.5)={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1.5)=-0.5$ and $g(-1.5)=-0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2.5,0)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-0.5)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1.5)=0.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1.5) & ={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-0.5)+(-0.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186100831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507485 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186100831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507509 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186100832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507423 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186100941 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507429 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186100941 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507458 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105467 1071 "![](images/83/830bc07f05846dcb09e360d6863387c9cc51eddc0027fe153cb2217cf7263a5a.png)\n\nThe hexagon $ABCDEF$ is regular. Find the value of $x$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186105467 1071 "![](images/83/830bc07f05846dcb09e360d6863387c9cc51eddc0027fe153cb2217cf7263a5a.png)\n\nThe hexagon $ABCDEF$ is regular. Find the value of $x.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105467 1074 "A regular hexagon is a $6$-sided polygon where all the interior angles have the same measure.\n\nThe sum of the measures of its interior angles is\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\times (6 - 2) = 720^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle BAF$ is\n\n$$\nm\\angle BAF = \\dfrac{720^\\circ}{6} = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nThe angles $\\angle QAB$, $\\angle BAF$, and $\\angle FAP$ form a straight angle. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠QAB + m∠BAF + m∠FAP &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + m∠BAF + x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x + 120^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186105467 1074 "A regular hexagon is a $6$-sided polygon where all the interior angles have the same measure.\n\nThe sum of the measures of its interior angles is \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\times (6 - 2) = 720^\\circ.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of  $\\angle BAF$  is \n\n$$\nm\\angle BAF  = \\dfrac{720^\\circ}{6} = 120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThe angles $\\angle QAB,$ $\\angle BAF,$ and $\\angle FAP$ form a straight angle. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QAB+m∠BAF+m∠FAP & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+m∠BAF+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+{120}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105468 1071 "In a regular polygon, all the exterior angles have a measure of $20^\\circ$. How many sides does the polygon have?" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186105468 1071 "In a regular polygon, all the exterior angles have a measure of $20^\\circ.$ How many sides does the polygon have?" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105468 1074 "The sum of the exterior angles of any polygon is $360^\\circ$.\n\nFor a regular polygon, all exterior angles have the same measure.\n\nTherefore, the number of exterior angles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{20^\\circ} = 18\\,\n$$\n\nThe number of sides, then, is also $18$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186105468 1074 "The sum of the exterior angles of any polygon is $360^\\circ.$ \n\nFor a regular polygon, all exterior angles have the same measure. \n\nTherefore, the number of exterior angles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{20^\\circ} = 18\\,.\n$$\n\nThe number of sides, then, is also $18.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105469 1074 "The shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular octagon and a regular triangle.\n\n![](images/04/04684884166fb5b87434b078962c78f71df65a0898e94c247b2763c6ba1368a3.png)\n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum to $360^\\circ$. In a regular octagon, there are $8$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{8} = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a regular triangle, there are $3$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{3} = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given angle has the following measure:\n\n$$\n45^\\circ + 120^\\circ = 165^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186105469 1074 "The shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular octagon and a regular triangle.\n\n![](images/04/04684884166fb5b87434b078962c78f71df65a0898e94c247b2763c6ba1368a3.png)\n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum to $360^\\circ.$ In a regular octagon, there are $8$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{8} = 45^\\circ .\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a regular triangle, there are $3$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{3} = 120^\\circ .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given angle has the following measure:\n\n$$\n45^\\circ + 120^\\circ = 165^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105470 1074 "A regular polygon is a polygon that has the following properties:\n\n- All sides have the same length (i.e., congruent).\n\n- All angles have the same measure (i.e., congruent).\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each polygon in turn:\n\n- Polygon I is regular. Its sides and angles are all congruent.\n\n- Polygon II is regular. Its sides and angles are all congruent.\n\n- Polygon III is not regular. Its sides and angles are *not* all congruent.\n\nTherefore the answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186105470 1074 "A regular polygon is a polygon that has the following properties:\n\n- All sides have the same length (i.e., congruent).\n- All angles have the same measure (i.e., congruent).\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each polygon in turn:\n\n- Polygon I is regular. Its sides and angles are all congruent.\n- Polygon II is regular. Its sides and angles are all congruent.\n- Polygon III is not regular. Its sides and angles are *not* all congruent.\n\nTherefore the answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535457 17592186045417 