$ sides. Therefore, the sum of its interior angles is\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\times (5 - 2) = 540^\\circ.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{540^\\circ}{5} = 108^\\circ,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a74a23a8dd760a31a0888eb694e36978c4767fd32bca6fae4fe8fe380c85e2e1.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186107165 1064 "![](images/8e/8e128a2320d2658cc61cf83a1871d09caa7e4527b684c2af9086f80a00ecc717.png)\n\nConsider the regular hexagon above. What is the measure of each *exterior* angle?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The exterior angles in a regular polygon are all congruent.\n\n- The sum of the exterior angles of *any* convex polygon equals $360^\\circ.$\n\nSo, to find the measure of the exterior angles of a regular polygon, we divide $360^\\circ$ by the number of sides.\n\nSince a regular hexagon has $n=6$ sides, the measure of each exterior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{6} = 60^\\circ,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f4538d9e2e09715fa3aa2035cd8b22cdc326b0ac02326551d193f26f1946473.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186107165 1064 "![](images/8e/8e128a2320d2658cc61cf83a1871d09caa7e4527b684c2af9086f80a00ecc717.png)\n\nConsider the regular hexagon above. What is the measure of each *exterior* angle?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The exterior angles in a regular polygon are all congruent.\n- The sum of the exterior angles of *any* convex polygon equals $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, to find the measure of the exterior angles of a regular polygon, we divide $360^\\circ$ by the number of sides.\n\nSince a regular hexagon has $n=6$ sides, the measure of each exterior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{6} = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f4538d9e2e09715fa3aa2035cd8b22cdc326b0ac02326551d193f26f1946473.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186114889 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114890 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186114895 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114896 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507432 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507521 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507435 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115992 1071 "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that a student gets $14$ correct answers on a true/false quiz with $25$ questions if the student answers all questions at random. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115992 1071 "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that a student gets $14$ correct answers on a true/false quiz with $25$ questions if the student answers all questions at random. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6554$ | $0.6591$ | $0.6628$ |\n| $0.6$ | $0.7257$ | $0.7291$ | $0.7324$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7881$ | $0.7910$ | $0.7939$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186115992 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(25)(0.5)=12.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(25)(0.5)(1-0.5)=6.25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(12.5,6.25).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=14).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5) & =P(\\frac{13.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.40\\le Z\\le 0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z\\le 0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.80)-Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.6554 \\\\  & =0.1327\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈0.133.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1327.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115993 1071 "It is known that $43%$ of the users of a newspaper's website purchase a digital subscription. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that in a random sample of $200$ users, more than $80$ but less than $105$ of them purchase a digital subscription. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1922\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1894\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1867\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2177\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2148\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2451\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9941\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9943\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9945\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9956\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9957\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9959\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9967\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9969\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186115993 1071 "It is known that $43%$ of the users of a newspaper's website purchase a digital subscription. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that in a random sample of $200$ users, more than $80$ but less than $105$ of them purchase a digital subscription. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$0.07$$0.08$$0.09$$-0.8$ $0.1922$ $0.1894$ $0.1867$ $-0.7$ $0.2206$ $0.2177$ $0.2148$ $-0.6$ $0.2514$ $0.2483$ $0.2451$ |  | $z$$0.02$$0.03$$0.04$$2.5$ $0.9941$ $0.9943$ $0.9945$ $2.6$ $0.9956$ $0.9957$ $0.9959$ $2.7$ $0.9967$ $0.9968$ $0.9969$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186115993 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.43.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.43 \\\\  & =86 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.43) \\\\  & =114 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.43)=86\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.43)(1-0.43)=49.02.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(86,49.02).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(80<X<105).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)=P(81\\le X\\le 104)≈P(80.5\\le Y\\le 104.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80.5\\le Y<104.5) & =P(\\frac{80.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{104.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.79\\le Z\\le 2.64) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z<-0.79) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z\\le -0.79) \\\\  & =Φ(2.64)-Φ(-0.79) \\\\  & =0.9959-0.2148 \\\\  & =0.7811\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)≈0.781.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of\n$0.7798.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186122792 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122794 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123194 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123196 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123200 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123204 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123658 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186123656 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507142 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123656 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507142 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123867 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123868 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123869 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123870 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123871 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506761 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506904 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507143 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507216 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123866 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507219 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186123866 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507143 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124206 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=-1,$ so the global maximum point is $(-1,4).$ \n\n![](images/f6/f6dec47fd3608523aeca83aac92b87407c3892598bf94e7bce14ae05881cb077.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124206 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum is $4$. The function reaches this value when $x=-1$, so the global maximum point is $(-1,4)$.\n\n![](images/f6/f6dec47fd3608523aeca83aac92b87407c3892598bf94e7bce14ae05881cb077.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186124207 1071 "The graph $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $1$.\n2. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $-4$.\n3. The function does not have any global extrema.\n\n![](images/ce/ceb091d2752790facfd1f63acd772f19b2f1f5808e2e90e6813f793831008bf7.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186124207 1071 "The graph $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following statements is true? \n\nI. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $1.$\n\nII. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $-4.$\n\nIII. The function does not have any global extrema.\n\n![](images/ce/ceb091d2752790facfd1f63acd772f19b2f1f5808e2e90e6813f793831008bf7.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124207 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ increases on the right-hand side of the graph, the function continues to increase without bound (as indicated by the arrow). So, the function does not reach a global maximum value.\n\n- Statement II is false. At first glance, it might seem that the point $(-4,-4)$ corresponds to the global minimum, but since $x=-4$ is not included in the domain (as indicated by the open circle), the function does *not* have a global minimum.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function does not have a global maximum and does not have a global minimum. Therefore, the function does not have any global extrema.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124207 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ increases on the right-hand side of the graph, the function continues to increase without bound (as indicated by the arrow). So, the function does not reach a global maximum value.\n- Statement II is false. At first glance, it might seem that the point $(-4,-4)$ corresponds to the global minimum, but since $x=-4$ is not included in the domain (as indicated by the open circle), the function does *not* have a global minimum.\n- Statement III is true. The function does not have a global maximum and does not have a global minimum. Therefore, the function does not have any global extrema.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186124372 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=0.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $0.$ The function reaches this value when $x=-3$ and when $x=1,$ so  the global minimum points are $(-3,0)$ and $(1,0).$\n\n![](images/41/413c4481cba4ee02fef831deb801f171b90f125937ac51e3ed283a5d94ccc5d7.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124372 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is $0$. The function reaches this value when $x=-3$ and when $x=1$, so the global minimum points are $(-3,0)$ and $(1,0)$.\n\n![](images/41/413c4481cba4ee02fef831deb801f171b90f125937ac51e3ed283a5d94ccc5d7.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186124502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507523 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124506 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507424 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124506 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507505 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507403 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507515 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124564 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507528 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124624 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507446 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124649 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507511 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124693 1050 420.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124699 1050 720.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121916 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186124887 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186124887 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}.\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186125032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125032 1071 "![](images/b5/b57f5f955178e8d10fff22c92d6cab0d009e4243ee90d0c48c36a67673316ae2.png)\n\nThe graph $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $4$.\n2. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $1$.\n3. The function does not have any global extrema." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186125032 1071 "![](images/b5/b57f5f955178e8d10fff22c92d6cab0d009e4243ee90d0c48c36a67673316ae2.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $4.$\n\nII. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $1.$\n\nIII. The function does not have any global extrema." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125032 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. At first glance, it might seem that the point $(1,4)$ corresponds to the global maximum, but since $x=1$ is not included in the domain (as indicated by the open circle), the function does *not* have a global maximum.\n\n- Statement II is true. The global minimum point is $(5,1),$ and so the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=1.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has a global minimum at the point $(5,1),$ and a global minimum is a type of global extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125099 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nThe minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-1.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-1.5.$ The function reaches this value at both $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n![](images/a2/a262ec40b439cceec6be37da8d590588012a0c3b9eed676dd09d0673540541b1.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125100 1071 "![](images/f2/f27757e4cfa1a17c436e7f99e05101f8596b53d7fea6add643ab32da4eb4f737.png)\n\nThe graph $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $2$.\n2. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $-2$.\n3. The function does not have any global extrema." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186125100 1071 "![](images/f2/f27757e4cfa1a17c436e7f99e05101f8596b53d7fea6add643ab32da4eb4f737.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true? \n\nI. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $2.$\n\nII. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $-2.$\n\nIII. The function does not have any global extrema." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125100 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ increases on the right-hand side or decreases on the left-hand side of the graph, the function continues to increase without bound (as indicated by the two arrows). So, the function does not reach a global maximum value.\n\n- Statement II is false. The global minimum points are $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-1).$ So the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-1.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has global minimums at the points $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-1),$ and a global minimum is a type of global extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125176 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $x=2,$ so the global maximum point is $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/ef/ef7ce52349abebebe9b1cfd210a611aac821aff01ae8c05925daa1540786e887.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125211 1071 "What is the projection of the intersection between the sphere $(x + 2)^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 9$ and the plane $z = 1$ onto the $xy$-plane." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186125211 1071 "What is the projection of the intersection between the sphere $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125211 1074 "To find the intersection of the sphere and the plane $z=1$, we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & =9 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $xy$-plane gives us the equation of the circle of radius $2\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-2,0).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125713 1064 "When we deal with functions, we are often interested in their largest or smallest values.\n\n- The **global maximum** of a function is the largest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum. It's also called the **absolute maximum.**\n\n- The **global minimum** of a function is the smallest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum. It's also called the **absolute minimum.**\n\nIf one or both exist, the global maximum and minimum values of a function are collectively called the **global extrema** of the function. \n\nFor example, in the graph below, the global maximum is $2$, while the global minimum is $-2$.\n\n![](images/22/2235cefbb995edcc085f96d8a5ff4e1ddd83f6345091c90ec3e0a7797fb9d8f3.png)\n\nThe point where a function reaches its global maximum/minimum is called the **global maximum/minimum point**.  So in this case, \n\n- $(-2,2)$ is the global maximum point, and\n\n- $(1,-2)$ is the global minimum point.\n\n**Watch out!** The *global maximum/minimum* is unique. It's a value of the function ($y$-coordinate on the graph). However, there can be several *global maximum/minimum points*. All of these will have the same $y$-coordinate (*global maximum/minimum*) but different $x$-coordinates." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125713 1064 "When we deal with functions, we are often interested in their largest or smallest values.\n\n- The **global maximum** of a function is the largest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum. It's also called the **absolute maximum.**\n- The **global minimum** of a function is the smallest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum. It's also called the **absolute minimum.**\n\nIf one or both exist, the global maximum and minimum values of a function are collectively called the **global extrema** of the function.\n\nFor example, in the graph below, the global maximum is $2$, while the global minimum is $-2$.\n\n![](images/22/2235cefbb995edcc085f96d8a5ff4e1ddd83f6345091c90ec3e0a7797fb9d8f3.png)\n\nThe point where a function reaches its global maximum/minimum is called the **global maximum/minimum point**. So in this case,\n\n- $(-2,2)$ is the global maximum point, and\n- $(1,-2)$ is the global minimum point.\n\n**Watch out!** The *global maximum/minimum* is unique. It's a value of the function ($y$-coordinate on the graph). However, there can be several *global maximum/minimum points*. All of these will have the same $y$-coordinate (*global maximum/minimum*) but different $x$-coordinates." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186125772 1064 "An **ellipsoid** is a quadric surface similar to a sphere but may be stretched (or compressed) in one or more directions.\n\nThe equation of an ellipsoid centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} = 1\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are the **semi-axes** of the ellipsoid parallel to the $x,y,$ and $z$ axes. \n\nNote that when $a=b=c,$ we get a sphere. Thus, a sphere is a special case of an ellipsoid.\n\nFor example, the ellipsoid centered at $O$ with the equation\n\n$$\nx ^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} + z ^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. \n\n![](images/e5/e5741ae47e163f600d74802d73b5727be382994fd906f490e42f8620fb0b9213.png)\n\nWe can find the intercepts of this ellipsoid with the $x,y,$ and $z$-axes as follows:\n\n- To find the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=z=0$. This gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{0^2}{4} + 0^2 = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the $x$-intercepts of the ellipsoid are $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- To find the $y$-intercepts, we set $x=z=0$. This gives\n\n$$\n0^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} + 0^2 = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThus, the $y$-intercepts of the ellipsoid are $(0,\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- To find the $z$-intercepts, we set $x=y=0$. This gives\n\n$$\n0^2 + \\dfrac{0^2}{4} + z^2 = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad z=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the $z$-intercepts of the ellipsoid are $(0,0,\\pm 1).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186125772 1064 "An **ellipsoid** is a quadric surface similar to a sphere but may be stretched (or compressed) in one or more directions.\n\nThe equation of an ellipsoid centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by $$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} = 1$$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are the **semi-axes** of the ellipsoid parallel to the $x,y,$ and $z$ axes.\n\nNote that when $a=b=c,$ we get a sphere. Thus, a sphere is a special case of an ellipsoid.\n\nFor example, the ellipsoid centered at $O$ with the equation\n\n$$x ^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} + z ^2 = 1$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e5741ae47e163f600d74802d73b5727be382994fd906f490e42f8620fb0b9213.png)\n\nWe can find the intercepts of this ellipsoid with the $x,y,$ and $z$-axes as follows:\n\n- To find the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=z=0$. This gives $$x^2 + \\dfrac{0^2}{4} + 0^2 = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 1.$$ Thus, the $x$-intercepts of the ellipsoid are $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n- To find the $y$-intercepts, we set $x=z=0$. This gives $$0^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} + 0^2 = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\pm 2.$$ Thus, the $y$-intercepts of the ellipsoid are $(0,\\pm 2,0).$\n- To find the $z$-intercepts, we set $x=y=0$. This gives $$0^2 + \\dfrac{0^2}{4} + z^2 = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad z=\\pm 1.$$ Thus, the $z$-intercepts of the ellipsoid are $(0,0,\\pm 1).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186128839 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the graph shown below?\n\n1. $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n2. $f(x) \\rightarrow -1$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$\n3. $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68123260b68bd3696d10948e30404534cc6b1b189ce6d8b680a2b616c9712ff3.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186128839 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the graph shown below?\n\nI. $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nII. $f(x) \\rightarrow -1$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$\n\nIII. $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68123260b68bd3696d10948e30404534cc6b1b189ce6d8b680a2b616c9712ff3.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186128839 1074 "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2$. Symbolically, $f(x) \\to 2 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow \\infty$. This means that $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f$. Therefore, I is true.\n- On the left side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-1$. Symbolically, $f(x) \\to -1 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -\\infty$. Therefore, II is also true.\n- The horizontal asymptotes of $f(x)$ are $y=2$ and $y=-1$. The line $y=0$ is not a horizontal asymptote because the function does not level off to $0$ on the right nor on the left. Therefore, III is false.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186128839 1074 "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ Symbolically, \n\n$$\nf(x) \\to 2 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow \\infty.\n$$\n\n This means that $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f.$ Therefore, I is true.\n\n- On the left side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-1.$ Symbolically, \n\n$$\nf(x) \\to -1 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -\\infty.\n$$\n\n Therefore, II is also true.\n\n- The horizontal asymptotes of $f(x)$ are $y=2$ and $y=-1$. The line $y=0$ is not a horizontal asymptote because the function does not level off to $0$ on the right nor on the left. Therefore, III is false.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186128840 1071 "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given below. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x) \\rightarrow a$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$ and $f(x) \\rightarrow b$ as $x  \\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n![](images/77/77305c6fdeb2b6f3cc0b427924e418938c07caac79843cc6992da799675aad4f.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186128840 1071 "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given below. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x) \\rightarrow a$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$ and $f(x) \\rightarrow b$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n\n![](images/77/77305c6fdeb2b6f3cc0b427924e418938c07caac79843cc6992da799675aad4f.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186128840 1074 "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ grows larger and larger without bound (as indicated by the arrow). We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = \\infty.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow -2 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b = -2.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186128840 1074 "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ grows larger and larger without bound (as indicated by the arrow). We can express this as follows:\n$f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow \\infty$\nTherefore, $a = \\infty$.\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-2$. We can express this as follows:\n$f(x) \\rightarrow -2 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow -\\infty$\nTherefore, $b = -2$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186129242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507407 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186129242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507527 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186129243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507474 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186129243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507492 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186129381 1050 420.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186130320 1064 "We are often interested in understanding how functions behave as their input becomes very large or very small. This is called the function's **end behavior.**\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/4d/4dfb6699ed366fb75ccd8f688cd802a49bc19b958ef3c7587b4fc7647323c7dc.png)\n\nThe key to understanding the end behavior is to ask the following two questions:\n\n1. What is the graph doing as $x$ becomes very large? ($x \\to \\infty$)\n2. What is the graph doing as $x$ becomes very small? ($x \\to -\\infty$)\n\nLet's now answer these questions.\n\n- First, consider $x \\to \\infty$. On the right of the graph, as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function value \"levels off\" to $1$. In other words, as $x$ approaches \"infinity,\" $f(x)$ approaches $1$. We can express this using mathematical notation as follows:\n$f(x) \\rightarrow 1 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow \\infty$![](images/56/567a1512a72228174cd258a43570407177e1fcef3eca77b4ac7c71cb951ff7a5.png)\n- Now, consider $x \\to -\\infty$. On the left of the graph, as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function does not \"level off.\" Instead, the value of $f(x)$ grows larger and larger without bound. In other words, as $x$ approaches \"negative infinity,\" $f(x)$ approaches \"infinity.\" We can express this using mathematical notation as follows:\n$f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow -\\infty$\n\n![](images/79/79f145a7459c782522ccc71d8aac83cfefc641a36d174b42ec1e8db6f8cb2696.png)\n\nWhen determining a function's end behavior, we ignore anything happening in the middle of the graph, as this is irrelevant to a function's end behavior.\n\nIf the graph \"levels off\" in any of the two cases above, we've found a **horizontal asymptote**! In the example above, $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote.\n\nMore formally, whenever $f(x)$ approaches some finite number $a$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$ or $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we say that $y=a$ is a horizontal asymptote of the function $y=f(x)$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186130320 1064 "We are often interested in understanding how functions behave as their input becomes very large or very small. This is called the function's **end behavior.**\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ below. \n\n![](images/4d/4dfb6699ed366fb75ccd8f688cd802a49bc19b958ef3c7587b4fc7647323c7dc.png)\n\nThe key to understanding the end behavior is to ask the following two questions:\n\n1\\. What is the graph doing as $x$ becomes very large? ($x \\to \\infty$)\n\n2\\. What is the graph doing as $x$ becomes very small? ($x \\to -\\infty$)\n\nLet's now answer these questions.\n\n- First, consider $x \\to \\infty.$ On the right of the graph, as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function value \"levels off\" to $1.$ In other words, as $x$ approaches \"infinity,\" $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ \n\nWe can express this using mathematical notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow 1 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow \\infty\n$$\n\n![](images/56/567a1512a72228174cd258a43570407177e1fcef3eca77b4ac7c71cb951ff7a5.png)\n\n- Now, consider $x \\to -\\infty.$ On the left of the graph, as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function does not \"level off.\" Instead, the value of $f(x)$ grows larger and larger without bound. In other words, as $x$ approaches \"negative infinity,\" $f(x)$ approaches \"infinity.\" \n\nWe can express this using mathematical notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow -\\infty\n$$\n\n![](images/79/79f145a7459c782522ccc71d8aac83cfefc641a36d174b42ec1e8db6f8cb2696.png)\n\nWhen determining a function's end behavior, we ignore anything happening in the middle of the graph, as this is irrelevant to a function's end behavior.\n\nIf the graph \"levels off\" in any of the two cases above, we've found a **horizontal asymptote**! In the example above, $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote. \n\nMore formally, whenever $f(x)$ approaches some finite number $a$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$ or $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we say that $y=a$ is a horizontal asymptote of the function $y=f(x).$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186159746 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159749 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507520 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507498 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507517 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162424 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nFind $E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 1\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186162424 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nFind $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186162424 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\sum }_{x}x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y).\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{2}{8}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\sum }_{x}x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186164010 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507004 13194139535457 1759218604541