gn=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{3t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186167161 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)} = \\frac{10}{s} - \\frac{1}{s - 3}, X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)} = \\frac{5}{s} + \\frac{1}{s - 3}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $+$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $e^{3t}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186167161 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{10}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =10{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot t} \\\\  & =10-{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{5}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =5{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+{e}^{3\\cdot t} \\\\  & =5+{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5+{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186173830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173831 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186173830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173832 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186173830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173835 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186173830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506903 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186173830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506988 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186173830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507198 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186173830 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506903 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507402 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507417 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507428 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507488 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176262 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186176262 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {4xy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nThe marginal CDF $F_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$F_{X}(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ g(x),\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1; 1 \\mid x > 1$.\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)$?" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186176262 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2x({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)={x}^{2}.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192543 1074 "We solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+13 & \\ge 3x-7 \\\\ 5x+13-3x & \\ge 3x-7-3x \\\\ 2x+13 & \\ge -7 \\\\ 2x+13-13 & \\ge -7-13 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ge -20 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge \\frac{-20}{2} \\\\ x & \\ge -10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\ge -10.$\n\nOur solution can be expressed using a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04b9a164fbff3b0603dcbd2b3283ad44ba480af91ae9af3dbd4ddcfe086c0752.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192543 1074 "We solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5x + 13 & \\ge 3x - 7 \\\\ 5x + 13 - 3x & \\ge 3x - 7 - 3x \\\\ 2x + 13 & \\ge - 7 \\\\ 2x + 13 - 13 & \\ge - 7 - 13 \\\\ 2x & \\ge - 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & \\ge - 20 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge \\frac{-20}{2} \\\\ x & \\ge - 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\ge -10\n$$\n\nOur solution can be expressed using a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04b9a164fbff3b0603dcbd2b3283ad44ba480af91ae9af3dbd4ddcfe086c0752.png)" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192544 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2-5y}{3} \\le 4$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192544 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2-5y}{3} \\le 4.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192544 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-5y}{3} & \\le 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2-5y}{3}) & \\le 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2-5y}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\le 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ 2-5y & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-5y & \\le 12 \\\\ 2-5y-2 & \\le 12-2 \\\\ -5y & \\le 10 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ y & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\ge -2.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192544 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 - 5y}{3} & \\le 4 \\\\ 3 \\cdot (\\frac{2 - 5y}{3}) & \\le 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 3 \\cdot (\\frac{2 - 5y}{3}) & \\le 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 2 - 5y & \\le 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 5y & \\le 12 \\\\ 2 - 5y - 2 & \\le 12 - 2 \\\\ -5y & \\le 10 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\ge\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\ge - 2 \\\\ y & \\ge - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny \\ge -2\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192545 1071 "Solve the inequality $4y -1 > \\dfrac {5y - 2} {3}$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192545 1071 "Solve the inequality $4y -1 > \\dfrac {5y - 2} {3}.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192545 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-1 & >\\frac{5y-2}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >3\\cdot (\\frac{5y-2}{3}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >\\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5y-2}{\\cancel{3}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >5y-2 \\\\ 12y-3 & >5y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t12y-3\t&> 5y-2 \\[5pt]\n\t\t12y-3-5y\t&> 5y-2-5y \\[5pt]\n\t\t7y-3\t&> {-2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t7y-3 +3\t&> {-2} +3 \\[5pt]\n\t\t7y\t\t&> {1} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{7y}{7}\t&> \\dfrac{1}7 \\[5pt]\n\t\ty\t\t&> \\dfrac{1}{7}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y > \\dfrac17.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192545 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4y - 1 & > \\frac{5y - 2}{3} \\\\ 3 \\cdot (4y - 1) & > 3 \\cdot (\\frac{5y - 2}{3}) \\\\ 3 \\cdot (4y - 1) & > 3 \\cdot (\\frac{5y - 2}{3}) \\\\ 3 \\cdot (4y - 1) & > 5y - 2 \\\\ 12y - 3 & > 5y - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 12y-3 &> 5y-2 \\\\[5pt] 12y-3-5y &> 5y-2-5y \\\\[5pt] 7y-3 &> {-2} \\\\[5pt] 7y-3 +3 &> {-2} +3 \\\\[5pt] 7y &> {1} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7y & > 1 \\\\ \\frac{7y}{7} & > \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{7y}{7} & > \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ y & > \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny > \\dfrac17\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192546 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3a-2}{5} > \\dfrac{4+6a}{2}$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192546 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3a-2}{5} > \\dfrac{4+6a}{2}.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192546 1074 "First, we clear the rational expressions by cross-multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3a - 2}{5} & > \\frac{4 + 6a}{2} \\\\ 2 \\cdot (3a - 2) & > 5 \\cdot (4 + 6a) \\\\ 6a - 4 & > 20 + 30a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6a - 4 & > 20 + 30a \\\\ 6a - 4 - 6a & > 20 + 30a - 6a \\\\ -4 & > 20 + 24a \\\\ -4 - 20 & > 20 + 24a - 20 \\\\ -24 & > 24a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & > 24a \\\\ \\frac{-24}{24} & > \\frac{24a}{24} \\\\ -1 & > \\frac{24a}{24} \\\\ -1 & > a \\\\ a & < - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a < -1$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192546 1074 "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a-2}{5} & >\\frac{4+6a}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{3a-2}{5}) & >10\\cdot (\\frac{4+6a}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (3a-2) & >5\\cdot (4+6a) \\\\ 6a-4 & >20+30a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-4 & >20+30a \\\\ 6a-4-30a & >20+30a-30a \\\\ -4-24a & >20 \\\\ -4-24a+4 & >20+4 \\\\ -24a & >24 \\\\ \\frac{-24a}{-24} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{24}{-24} \\\\ a & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a < -1.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507437 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507516 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507426 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507456 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507503 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507475 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507491 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192823 1039 "Inequalities With Rational Expression and Variables on Both Sides" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192823 1039 "Inequalities With Rational Expressions and Variables on Both Sides" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507419 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507467 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507477 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507506 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1039 "Solving Inequalities by Clearing Two Rational Expressions" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1039 "Solving Linear Inequalities Using Cross-Multiplication" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186192910 1050 780.0 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186193914 1064 "Let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n7x -2 < 5x - 18\n$$\n\nAs usual, we solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle. We start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7x - 2 & < 5x - 18 \\\\ 7x - 2 - 5x & < 5x - 18 - 5x \\\\ 2x - 2 & < - 18\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x - 2 & < - 18 \\\\ 2x - 2 + 2 & < - 18 + 2 \\\\ 2x & < - 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < - 16 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & < \\frac{-16}{2} \\\\ x & < - 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x < -8$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186193914 1064 "Let's consider the following inequality: \n\n$$\n7x -2 < 5x - 18\n$$\n\nAs usual, we solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle. We start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-2 & <5x-18 \\\\ 7x-2-5x & <5x-18-5x \\\\ 2x-2 & <-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & <-18 \\\\ 2x-2+2 & <-18+2 \\\\ 2x & <-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <-16 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & <\\frac{-16}{2} \\\\ x & <-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x < -8.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186193915 1064 "Now, let's solve an inequality that contains a rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2y-1}{3} \\lt 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-1}{3} & <5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{3}) & <3\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{3}}) & <15 \\\\ 2y-1 & <15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-1 & <15 \\\\ 2y-1+1 & <15+1 \\\\ 2y & <16 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & <\\frac{16}{2} \\\\ y & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y <  8.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186193915 1064 "Now, let's solve an inequality that contains a rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2y-1}{3} \\lt 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2y - 1}{3} & < 5 \\\\ 3 \\cdot (\\frac{2y - 1}{3}) & < 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\ 3 \\cdot (\\frac{2y - 1}{3}) & < 15 \\\\ 2y - 1 & < 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2y - 1 & < 15 \\\\ 2y - 1 + 1 & < 15 + 1 \\\\ 2y & < 16 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & < \\frac{16}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & < \\frac{16}{2} \\\\ y & < 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y < 8$." 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186193915 1067 "Clearing Rational Expression in Linear Inequalities" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186193915 1067 "Clearing Rational Expressions in Linear Inequalities" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186193916 1064 "Finally, let's consider an inequality that contains rational expressions on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{4} \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we clear the rational expressions by cross-multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x + 1}{4} & \\le \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 2 \\cdot (x + 1) & \\le 4 \\cdot x \\\\ 2x + 2 & \\le 4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x + 2 & \\le 4x \\\\ 2x + 2 - 2x & \\le 4x - 2x \\\\ 2 & \\le 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\le 2x \\\\ \\frac{2}{2} & \\le \\frac{2x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2} & \\le \\frac{2x}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le x \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\geq 1\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186193916 1064 "Finally, let's consider an inequality that contains rational expressions on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{4} \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThe rational expressions on both sides of this inequality have positive denominators $4$ and $2.$ The least common multiple of $4$ and $2$ is ${\\color{red}{4}}.$\nThus, we can clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality ${\\color{red}{4}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4} & \\le \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{4}) & \\le 4\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ x+1 & \\le 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\le 2x \\\\ x+1-2x & \\le 2x-2x \\\\ 1-x & \\le 0 \\\\ 1-x-1 & \\le 0-1 \\\\ -x & \\le -1 \\\\ \\frac{-x}{(-1)} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\geq 1.$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186193916 1067 "Clearing Two Rational Expressions in Linear Inequalities" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186193916 1067 "Cross-Multiplication in Linear Inequalities" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186221456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507450 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186221456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507482 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186221457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507448 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186221457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507508 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186234246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507314 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282057 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282162 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282478 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282635 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283064 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506768 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506838 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506998 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507119 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284231 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507192 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284231 1078 #edb/ref 17592186282478 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186284231 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506838 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284483 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186284483 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321210 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321380 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321641 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322946 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506710 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506748 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506968 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186323612 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506978 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186323612 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321380 13194139535457 17592186045417 false] [17592186323612 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506710 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186368362 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=4 \\\\ \\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{4}{\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" 13194139535457 17592186045417 true] [17592186368362 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\